Measure a forest's shape without carrying anything that identifies it (P-280)

Step 2a of the P-280 work order. SecureAD.Measure --shape-profile BUNDLE
[OUTPUT] reads a sealed capture and writes counts, distributions and the GUIDs
and SIDs that mean the same thing in every Active Directory. 6,599 bytes on the
bench, small enough to carry and harmless enough to be allowed out of the
environment it was taken in.

WHY. Every figure P-280 rests on came off a synthetic bundle this codebase
generated, so it can only hold the assumptions the code already held. That is
F-S44-2: the captured attribute catalogue had three sound suites agreeing with
each other in both directions while fifteen attributes nobody had written a rule
for were silently missing. The owner cannot export a bundle - he works only
inside the production environment - so the anchor has to be something small
enough to carry out.

WHAT MAY BE WRITTEN DOWN is decided in one place, ShapeProfile.Publishable, and
not field by field, because a field added later could otherwise leak by being
written in a different style while every check still passed. A schema GUID is
named only if SchemaGuidCatalog.Embedded knows it, and deliberately not if the
bundle's own captured schema does: the captured layer re-reads the base schema
as well as the extensions, so asking it would answer yes for contoso-PayrollId
and print it. SchemaGuidSource's own summary already drew that line. A trustee is
named only if WellKnownSidCatalog knows it, which excludes every S-1-5-21 domain
SID as a consequence of admitting only the universal set rather than as a prefix
case somebody had to remember. The bundle is reported by folder name alone and
the executable path is not reported at all, because on the owner's machine both
carry his account name.

WHAT CANNOT BE NAMED IS STILL COUNTED. Distinct and total occurrences of schema
GUIDs outside the shipped catalogue, and occurrences whose trustee is not
well-known, are reported as numbers. A profile that discarded what it could not
name would pass a leak gate perfectly and tell a generator nothing about how much
of the forest is locally extended. Refusing to name a thing and refusing to count
it are different acts and only the first is required.

The distinguished name is read in pass one for three things and kept for none:
its byte length, its comma depth, and its DC= suffix as a dictionary key whose
keys are discarded and whose values are emitted sorted and unlabelled. The name
cannot reach the document by any route, and ObjectsPerDomain being
IReadOnlyList<long> means the domain key is structurally unable to, which is
stronger than a check.

Distributions rather than averages, because an average is what got the occurrence
count wrong: 344 million was entries times reuse, exact only if a descriptor's
entry count and its reuse count are unrelated, and the measurement came in 3.8%
under. Percentiles are nearest-rank, so every value reported is one the forest
really holds. The entries-against-reuse grid is the one thing two separate
distributions cannot say, and it is where that assumption failed.

Descriptors are parsed out of their stored bytes exactly as
ObjectPermissions.EntriesOf parses them. Reading descriptor_entry would describe
the canonical projection, which is sorted and deduplicated by ace_hash, and a
generator taught to match that would build a forest whose DACLs never repeat a
grant. The directory's do.

THE FIRST OUTPUT IS ITSELF THE FINDING. On next/artifacts/p279-after: entries per
descriptor p50, p99 and max all 521; name bytes p50, p99 and max all 97; one
domain; objects per descriptor never above 3; zero occurrences carrying an object
type; zero carrying S-1-5-10; zero carrying a well-known trustee; zero unreadable
or partly parsed descriptors. A forest where the median, the 99th percentile and
the maximum agree to the unit is not a forest, it is a constant. The two figures
P-280's largest claim rests on - 72% of production occurrences carrying an
object-type GUID, and normalising saving three times what per-descriptor keying
saves - have never been checked against anything.

TWENTY-FIVE CHECKS, and the two that mattered most were found by mutation to be
measuring nothing. The leak gate is nine markers across four routes into the
profile, with four negative controls: the published GUID and its name are present
so the absences cannot pass against an empty document, the secret GUID and the
secret trustee are each proved to have been counted, and Publishable is held
directly to the shipped catalogue. Six mutations with explicit rebuilds either
side. Four were killed at once - naming an unknown GUID, naming every trustee,
discarding instead of counting, and zeroing the no-descriptor count.

TWO SURVIVED. The path check asserted that C:\Users\... was absent from the
document, and JSON escapes a backslash as a pair, so the raw path never appeared
literally however much of it was emitted: the check passed with the whole path in
the output. Rewritten against a backslash-free marker in the parent folder name,
with a negative control that the run's own folder name is still reported. The
percentile check asserted only p50 and p90, and shifting the rank from
ceiling(f*n)-1 to floor(f*(n-1)) leaves both unchanged on ten values; strengthened
to assert p99, where the definitions part and where a real forest's tail lives.
Both corrected checks were then confirmed to kill the mutation they had slept
through.

Also records in the specification what the bench measured, so nothing that
follows is believed on its authority, and notes on P-285 that measuring the SELF
inflation now needs only a production run.

Verified: the bench bundle is byte-identical after profiling, and the profile
contains no account name, no path, no S-1-5-21 SID and no file name. Its
occurrence total of 17,164,866 agrees exactly with --judgement-forecast's, which
is a separately written pass over the same evidence.
